Crypto.c is my contribution to the world of cryptogram puzzle solvers.  I wroteit a few months ago to encode and solve fortunes, and to decode the cryptogramsfound in _Games_ magazine.  Included is an example multi-puzzle file which willlet you try out the program.  After compilation, type "crypto puzzles" and typea "?" for a brief summary of commands.  Consult the manpage for more info.The program is written using curses, and as such is (hopefully) quite portable.Edit the Makefile if the curses library link is not "-lcurses -ltermcap", or ifyour system uses strrchr() rather than rindex().Crypto helps those who enjoy solving cryptograms by:    o	Encoding regular text    o	Using pre-encoded files which may or may not include the solution    o	Interactivley placing letters into the puzzle    o	Non-interactivley encoding/decoding puzzle text    o	Searching /usr/dict/words for possible matches of a particular word    o	Creating/accessing multi-puzzle filesFor example, execute the command:	fortune | crypto -e >>puzzten times, then the command:	crypto puzzto solve all ten puzzles at your leisure.  You can switch from puzzle topuzzle, and save the current state of any one or all ten puzzles.  You caneven cheat by having the program correct one (or more) letters in the puzzle.-- Wayne Davison					      ...amdahl!drivax!davison=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=   bcd'f bf g hif ij jkd fi cihlm fnmcm fompmdqikchr aidqmojkh sortfiuogpc?